  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 AM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions/Today:\n\nHigh pressure is jaunting  off to the southeast allowing for broad \nsoutherly winds and warm air advection to take hold of the region. \nRadar this morning shows a few light echoes cruising across northern \nMN, but no reports of rain reaching the ground with this activity. \nMore vigorous activity is expected later this morning as a strong \nlow level jet is expected to develop across the Northern Plains \nwhich will increase the chances of some elevated storms to develop \nin the Red River Valley. Strong instability (1000-2000 J/kg of \nMUCAPE) with a bulk shear of 40-50kts could lead to severe storms \nthat generate large hail. The current suite of high res guidance \nfavors this being farther west outside of our CWA where the HREF is \nhighlighting some swaths of higher updraft helicity. High res \nguidance has come into better agreement with the recent runs \nsuggesting that this morning activity will largely stay west of the \nregion and possibly clip into Koochiching during the afternoon \nhours. Still need to keep a watchful eye out west this morning as \nthe severe risk is still categorized as a marginal (level 1 out of \n5).\n\nActivity is expected to ramp back up in evening. Despite the morning \nstorm activity, the region will see ample recovery with southerly \nflow promoting warm advection and pushing MLCAPE upwards of 1000-\n2000 J/kg. A cold front will move east through the late afternoon \nand evening period triggering convection. Bulk shear will still be \nbountiful with an upper level jet rounding and upper level low \nmoving into Manitoba. Supercells will be possible capable of \nproducing large hail and damaging winds. A tornado can't be ruled \nout at this time, but the better streamwise vorticity looks to be \nover the Red River Valley, which then fades as the storm mode \nbecomes more linear as it tracks into our CWA. Showers and storms \nwill continue to move across the region through the overnight period \nwith the severe risk fading in the early morning hours. Thereafter, \nshowers and a few weaker storms will continue through the region \nTuesday as the cold front pushes west to east. \n\nMid week:\n\nHigh pressure over the region for Wednesday temperatures staying in \nthe 70s. Temperatures will slowly increase through the end of the \nwork week with intermittent rain chances possible as the upper level \npattern turns more zonal.",
